  "query_preview": "/FRIDAY THROUGH TUESDAY/... \nStrong easterly winds are expected to develop across southern and\ncentral Arizona by Friday morning with guidance showing H8 winds\nof 30-45 kts across southern and much of central Arizona. The best\ncorridor for strong winds looks to be across southeast Arizona\ninto southern Gila County and through Pinal, southern Maricopa, \nand Yuma Counties. Occasional gusty winds of 25-40 mph are \nexpected before sunrise in this area before increasing to advisory\nlevel around or just after sunrise Friday morning. Wind \nAdvisories have been issued for the aforementioned areas for \nFriday morning into the afternoon with wind gusts in excess of 40 \nmph at times. The peak of the winds should occur mid to late \nmorning Friday with some gusts up to 50 mph possible. Areas to the\nnorth, including Phoenix, should stay below advisory level but\ngusts of 30-40 mph will be likely during the latter half of the\nmorning Friday. Patchy blowing dust will also be a concern on \nFriday, particularly across Pinal County. Winds should eventually \ndrop off some for most areas during the afternoon hours Friday, \nbut advisory levels winds should continue to be a concern through \nthe rest of Friday and even into Saturday across higher terrain \nareas east and southeast of Phoenix.\n\nWinds Saturday will again be elevated with gusts of 25-35 mph for\nmuch of southern and central Arizona, but any advisory level \nwinds are likely to be limited southeast Arizona. Aside from the\nwind concern, guidance is suggesting some lower end shower\npotential later Friday through Saturday. The placement of the\ncut-off low to our southwest starting Friday is expected to advect\na good amount of mid and upper level moisture into the area. Some\nhigh-based light showers may become possible across southern\nArizona as early as Friday afternoon, but little if any\nmeasurable rainfall is expected. These low-end (10-20%) light \nshower chances may stick around through Saturday as the cut-off \nlow begins to drift back through our region, but most locations \nwill struggle to get anything more than a trace to a few \nhundredths of an inch.\n\nEarly next week is likely to be a transition period with weak\nupper level ridging briefly taking over and overall dry\nconditions. After a brief dip in temperatures on Saturday, daytime\nhighs should trend closer to normal for early next week. Looking\nout even further, ensemble guidance is hinting at another deep \nPacific trough moving into the Western U.S. early next week and\npotentially reaching our region during the middle part of next\nweek. This next weather system could bring more rain chances and\nanother cool down during the latter half of next week.",
